-- Example: schedule room-count truing.
--
-- Like the card check next door, this posts to the Next.js app rather than an
-- Edge Function: raising a subscription's quantity needs the same Stripe client
-- the rest of billing uses (src/lib/billing/*).
--
-- ONE cron entry drives two things, in this order, every run:
--   1. Email anyone newly found billing for fewer rooms than they run.
--   2. Correct anyone whose seven-day grace has expired AND who was emailed
--      about that exact number.
--
-- The ordering is the safety property. A property is never charged more in an
-- invocation where its warning did not go out — trueUpOne refuses to act until
-- room_shortfall_notified_rooms matches the current measurement, so a Resend
-- outage or a customer with no email address delays the correction rather than
-- delivering a surprise invoice.
--
-- Daily is deliberate. The grace period is measured in days, so a faster cron
-- buys nothing, and this is the one job in the system that increases what a
-- customer pays — a low frequency keeps the blast radius of a bug small and
-- gives you a whole day to notice it in the logs.
--
-- Prereqs: extensions `pg_cron`, `pg_net`; Vault secrets. Reuses the SAME
-- billing_cron_secret as the card check.
--
-- 0) Ensure extensions exist:
--    create extension if not exists pg_net;
--    create extension if not exists pg_cron;
--
-- 1) Secrets (both already exist if the card check is scheduled):
--    select vault.create_secret('https://maya.your-domain.com', 'maya_app_url');
--    select vault.create_secret('<long-random-string>', 'billing_cron_secret');
--
-- 2) App env (Vercel → Environment Variables, Production + Preview):
--    BILLING_CRON_SECRET, STRIPE_SECRET_KEY, SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY,
--    NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL, RESEND_API_KEY, MAYA_INVITE_REDIRECT_BASE
--
--    RESEND_API_KEY and MAYA_INVITE_REDIRECT_BASE are not optional here. Without
--    either, the warning cannot be sent — and because nothing is corrected
--    unwarned, the sweep will measure, log, and change nobody's bill.
--
-- 3) Schedule (09:20 UTC daily):
--    select cron.schedule(
--      'billing-room-truing',
--      '20 9 * * *',
--      $$
--      select net.http_post(
--        url := (select decrypted_secret from vault.decrypted_secrets where name = 'maya_app_url')
--               || '/api/billing/room-truing',
--        headers := jsonb_build_object(
--          'Content-Type', 'application/json',
--          'x-billing-cron-secret', (select decrypted_secret from vault.decrypted_secrets where name = 'billing_cron_secret')
--        ),
--        body := jsonb_build_object('scheduled_at', now())
--      ) as request_id;
--      $$
--    );
--
-- Inspect:    select jobid, jobname, schedule, active from cron.job;
-- Runs log:   select * from cron.job_run_details order by start_time desc limit 20;
-- Unschedule: select cron.unschedule('billing-room-truing');
--
-- Who is currently short, and where each one is in the process. notified_rooms
-- matching measured_rooms is what makes a row eligible for correction:
--   select hotel_id, billed_rooms, measured_rooms, measured_rooms_at,
--          room_shortfall_since, room_shortfall_notified_at,
--          room_shortfall_notified_rooms, room_corrected_at
--     from hotel_subscriptions
--    where room_shortfall_since is not null
--       or room_corrected_at is not null
--    order by room_shortfall_since nulls last;
--
-- Properties paying for MORE rooms than they run. Deliberately never corrected
-- automatically — a room type off for a refurbishment would cut the bill without
-- anyone asking — but worth a look before a renewal:
--   select hotel_id, billed_rooms, measured_rooms
--     from hotel_subscriptions
--    where measured_rooms is not null and measured_rooms < billed_rooms;
